{"version":3,"kind":"Article","sha256":"e5d789febad4d79ad731dc6e2e9888000b631af39b70e201ff0e329fcbcbb3a9","slug":"pages.p198-m15-overview-2-of-4","location":"/pages/p198-m15-overview-2-of-4.md","dependencies":[],"frontmatter":{"title":"The Four Steps of a Hypothesis Test","content_includes_title":false,"authors":[{"nameParsed":{"literal":"City College of San Francisco","given":"City College","non_dropping_particle":"of","family":"San Francisco"},"name":"City College of San Francisco","id":"contributors-myst-generated-uid-0"}],"license":{"content":{"id":"CC-BY-NC-SA-4.0","url":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/","name":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","CC":true}},"numbering":{"title":{"enabled":true,"offset":0,"enumerator":"%s."}},"abbreviations":{"ANOVA":"Analysis of Variance","CI":"Confidence Interval","CLT":"Central Limit Theorem","EDA":"Exploratory Data Analysis","IQR":"Interquartile Range","SD":"Standard Deviation","SE":"Standard Error","SRS":"Simple Random Sample","INCREASE":null,"INCREASED":null,"DECREASES":null,"OBSERVED":null,"ROSE":null},"enumerator":"178.","thumbnail":"/textbook/build/m15-logic-flow-097a87528ac5381239f5818ca6cd700c.svg","exports":[{"format":"md","filename":"p198-m15-overview-2-of-4.md","url":"/textbook/build/p198-m15-overview-2--bea30d5faa50c5aeca4b4eb25581d408.md"}]},"mdast":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"block","children":[{"type":"admonition","children":[{"type":"admonitionTitle","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Example: Concentration of a Chemical in a Drug","position":{"start":{"line":3,"column":1},"end":{"line":3,"column":1}},"key":"szjN96sVJz"}],"key":"bW19c5FMpR"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":6,"column":1},"end":{"line":6,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"A certain prescription allergy medicine is supposed to contain an average of 245 parts per million (ppm) of a certain chemical. If the concentration is higher than 245 ppm, the drug will likely cause unpleasant side effects, and if the concentration is below 245 ppm, the drug may be ineffective. The manufacturer wants to check whether the mean concentration in a large shipment is the required 245 ppm or not. To this end, a random sample of 64 portions from the large shipment is tested, and it is found that the sample mean concentration is 250 ppm with a sample standard deviation of 12 ppm. Let’s analyze this example according to the four steps of hypothesis testing we outlined on the previous page:","position":{"start":{"line":6,"column":1},"end":{"line":6,"column":1}},"key":"Ky5DZu0pkj"}],"key":"B1MENGZtgv"},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"spread":false,"position":{"start":{"line":8,"column":1},"end":{"line":17,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"listItem","spread":true,"position":{"start":{"line":8,"column":1},"end":{"line":12,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":8,"column":1},"end":{"line":8,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"emphasis","position":{"start":{"line":8,"column":1},"end":{"line":8,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Stating the claims:","position":{"start":{"line":8,"column":1},"end":{"line":8,"column":1}},"key":"qeyHDYqdna"}],"key":"pM0K76obL3"},{"type":"text","value":" Note that again, claim 1 basically says: “There is nothing unusual about this shipment; the mean concentration is the required 245 ppm.” This claim is challenged by the manufacturer, who wants to check whether that is, indeed, the case or not.","position":{"start":{"line":8,"column":1},"end":{"line":8,"column":1}},"key":"xIbLLFU5sn"}],"key":"hM1ZyiJKn1"},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"spread":false,"position":{"start":{"line":10,"column":1},"end":{"line":12,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"listItem","spread":true,"position":{"start":{"line":10,"column":1},"end":{"line":10,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"emphasis","position":{"start":{"line":10,"column":1},"end":{"line":1